Tie each source-location assertion to the line it captures, not to a literal

The conversion rewrites a consumer's includes into imports, which changes how
many lines stand above the code. Every source location the file reports moves
with them, and a test that names the line outright then fails by exactly that
shift:

  ec_location_test.cpp(36): test 'ec.location().line() == 27'
                            ('26' == '27') failed

The line a BOOST_CURRENT_LOCATION sits on is the thing under test, so the
assertion asks __LINE__ for it rather than repeating a number that was only
ever true for one arrangement of the file. Verified against UNPATCHED upstream
headers as well: the patched tests pass there too, so this is not a test bent
into passing.

